<BaseLayout title="Rods in Vancouver — Huanyu Chen"
  description="SIGGRAPH 2025 in Vancouver: presenting ANIME-Rod, a week of harbor views from the convention centre, Richmond takeout, and an Air Canada strike that kept me an extra day.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · August 2025</p>
  <h1>Rods in Vancouver</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">SIGGRAPH 2025, presenting ANIME-Rod, and a strike that kept me in Vancouver an extra day.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>8 days · August 9 – 16, 2025</strong><br />
    YVR → Richmond → downtown Vancouver (SIGGRAPH 2025, Vancouver Convention Centre) → Yaletown → Richmond → YVR<br />
    one paper presented · five days of conference · four beds in three neighborhoods · no rental car, mostly the
    Canada Line and my feet · one cancelled flight · one sequel
  </div>

  <p>
    A year ago, on the show floor of SIGGRAPH 2024 in Denver, a booth was already selling the next summer:
    <em>SIGGRAPH 2025, Vancouver, August 10 to 14</em>. I photographed it at the time. Twelve months
    later I was in Vancouver with a paper to present. That Denver week was the prologue to a two-week road trip, which
    is written up <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">here</a>. This one is a sequel, and like most sequels it is shorter,
    stays in one city, and has more talking in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    As before, everything below is reconstructed from the camera roll: the time and place stamped on each photo, lined
    up in order. A conference week is a strange thing to reconstruct that way. The camera mostly records what happens
    <em>between</em> the sessions: the view out of a window during a coffee break, lunch, a sunset on the way out of
    the building. The sessions themselves leave almost no trace, apart from one very well documented half hour on
    Tuesday afternoon. Where the record is thin, I have kept the text thin too.
  </p>

  <h2>Days 1–2: Landing, and opening day</h2>
  <p>
    I came in to <strong>Vancouver International Airport</strong> on the evening of Saturday, August 9th. The first
    photo of the trip is from a terminal window at 8:32 pm. An Air Canada jet sits nose-in at its gate, the apron
    in front of it is crowded with baggage carts and cargo containers stenciled with the names of airlines from the
    other side of the Pacific, and beyond the runways a low, dark line of mountains stands against a peach-colored sky.
    It is a good first view of the city's geography: flat delta land in front, mountains behind, and the sun going down
    late, the way it does at 49 degrees north in August.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0810.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0810.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Days 1–2: landing at YVR, a first night in Richmond, then into the city for SIGGRAPH." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 1–2: landing at YVR, a first night in Richmond, then into the city for SIGGRAPH.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'yvr-arrival.webp'} alt="An Air Canada jet at a gate at Vancouver airport at sunset, with baggage carts in front and mountains on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>YVR, 8:32 pm, August 9th.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The first night was in <strong>Richmond</strong>, the city just south of Vancouver that shares Sea Island and
    the airport with it. Richmond sits on Lulu Island in the delta of the Fraser River, so flat and so close to sea level
    that it is ringed by dikes. It is also, by reputation and in practice, one of the best places to eat Chinese food in
    North America: a large share of its residents have roots in Hong Kong, Taiwan and mainland China, and the
    restaurants along its main roads follow. My first dinner, late that night, came in a paper bowl: a fish soup with
    pickled mustard greens, a side of rice noodles to drop into it, and a small cup of steamed milk custard for
    afterwards. After a day of travel it was exactly right.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'fish-soup.webp'} alt="Takeout containers of fish soup with pickled greens, plain rice noodles and a milk custard." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The first dinner: fish soup, rice noodles and milk custard.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Into the city</h3>
  <p>
    Sunday was opening day. Around half past noon I crossed the <strong>Middle Arm of the Fraser</strong>, one of the
    channels the river splits into before it reaches the sea. Under a perfectly clear sky the water was wide and
    glassy, with gravel docks and silos on its banks and, far off on the horizon, the faint towers of downtown
    Vancouver. It is a working river: logs, barges and gravel still move along it, and the delta it built is the
    ground the whole southern half of the metro area stands on.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'middle-arm.webp'} alt="View over a bridge railing across the wide Middle Arm of the Fraser River, with industrial docks and distant towers under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Middle Arm of the Fraser, on the way into the city.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By 1:19 pm I was inside the <strong>Vancouver Convention Centre</strong>, standing in front of the "All Level
    Overview": the map that every attendee photographs on day one and consults on every day after. SIGGRAPH has come to
    Vancouver several times in recent years, and the building is a big part of the reason. Its West Building opened in
    2009, is built partly out over the water of Coal Harbour, and is finished inside in long slats of pale wood that run
    across the ceilings like the ribs of a boat. On top is a living roof of grasses and native plants, about six acres
    of it, which from the North Shore makes the building look like a piece of meadow that slid down into the harbor.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'floor-map.webp'} alt="A tall SIGGRAPH 2025 floor-map sign titled All Level Overview, under a slatted wooden ceiling." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Day one, the map.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    And then there are the windows. The convention centre is wrapped in glass, and almost every corridor ends in a
    view. A few minutes after the floor map came the first one. On one side, at <strong>Canada Place</strong>, a
    Holland America cruise ship lay alongside the pier, "We ♥ Alaska" painted on its bow, with people strolling the
    promenade deck of the pier below it. Canada Place was built as Canada's pavilion for Expo 86, and its roof of
    white fabric sails has been the city's postcard ever since; today it is mostly a cruise terminal, the starting
    point for ships heading up the Inside Passage. On the other side, across Burrard Inlet, the
    <strong>North Shore Mountains</strong> rose straight out of the water, with the dark green edge of Stanley Park
    at the left. It was hard to believe this was the view from a conference hallway.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canada-place-ship.webp'} alt="A large cruise ship docked at Canada Place, beside a tower and the pier's promenade, under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A cruise ship at Canada Place, from the convention centre.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'north-shore-window.webp'} alt="Floor-to-ceiling windows looking across Burrard Inlet to the North Shore Mountains." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The North Shore Mountains, through the convention centre's glass.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The evening belonged to the big hall and to SIGGRAPH's traditional opening ritual, the
    <strong>Technical Papers Fast Forward</strong>, where every paper in the conference gets well under a minute to
    make its pitch, one after another, for a couple of hours. It is part trailer reel and part endurance sport, and the
    audience reacts to the jokes like a comedy crowd. The record has a short video at 7:19 pm and a photo at 8:52 pm: a
    dark auditorium with rows of heads in silhouette, stage lights raking through the haze, a glowing SIGGRAPH logo, a
    wall of glowing blocks, and on the screen a slide from a paper in a session called "Rigid Bodies &amp; Contacts",
    which wasn't due to be presented in full until Thursday.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'evening-session.webp'} alt="A dark auditorium with stage lighting, a glowing SIGGRAPH logo sculpture and a slide on a large screen." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Technical Papers Fast Forward, Sunday evening.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Coming out at nine o'clock, the sky over the North Shore had caught fire. From the plaza outside the West Building,
    the mountains were a black cutout against a band of orange and red, the tall street lamps of the plaza leaning over
    the scene like a row of herons. Turn around, and downtown's glass towers stood dark against the last yellow light,
    with the four white struts of the <strong>Olympic Cauldron</strong> lit blue beneath them. The cauldron was built
    for the 2010 Winter Games, which used this building as their media centre, and it still stands on the plaza,
    its flame lit only on special occasions, like a piece of sculpture waiting for its cue. On the same plaza,
    Douglas Coupland's <strong>Digital Orca</strong>, a killer whale built out of black and white blocks, stood
    against the glow.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'plaza-sunset.webp'} alt="A vivid orange and red sunset over the dark North Shore Mountains, seen from the convention centre plaza with tall lamp posts." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset over the North Shore, 9:01 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cauldron-dusk.webp'} alt="Downtown towers at dusk above a plaza with the angular white Olympic Cauldron lit in blue." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The 2010 Olympic Cauldron and downtown at dusk.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Days 3–4: The talk</h2>
  <p>
    Monday afternoon I sat in the audience at a papers session on cloth and knits: one talk on scheduling cloth
    simulations across mixed hardware, and one on rendering knitted fabric in real time, yarn by yarn, ending on a
    knitted teapot. Later, at 4:33, I found a quiet corner of the convention centre with two dark armchairs facing the
    glass, and outside it a different ship at Canada Place. This one was a Disney liner, black hull, red funnels, gold trim, as if a
    cartoon had docked. Cruise ships come and go from Canada Place almost daily in summer, and every day of the
    week the view out of the window had a new one in it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0812.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0812.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Days 3–4: the convention centre, Korean hot pot in the West End, a Sichuan lunch off Robson, and the talk in the West Building." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 3–4: harbor views, hot pot, and the talk.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'disney-ship.webp'} alt="A Disney cruise ship with red funnels docked at Canada Place, seen through tall windows past two armchairs." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Monday's ship.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner was a short walk up into the West End, at a Korean place where the centerpiece arrived in a wide steel pan
    on a burner: a red, milky broth full of enoki and sliced mushrooms, tofu and noodles, heaped with spring onions,
    with little dishes of chili paste, sliced jalapeño and pickled garlic on the side. Around it the table filled up
    with banchan, rice in steel bowls, lettuce for wrapping and a hot plate of pork. Downtown Vancouver's Korean and
    Japanese restaurants are a well-known feature of the streets around Robson, and this one did not let the reputation
    down.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'korean-hotpot.webp'} alt="A wide steel pan of red, creamy hot pot with mushrooms, noodles and spring onions, with small side dishes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Korean hot pot in the West End.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Tuesday: ANIME-Rod</h3>
  <p>
    Tuesday was the day. Lunch, a little after one, was Sichuan, a few blocks off Robson Street, with a table of
    fellow attendees. The photo that survives shows a broad white bowl of fish poached in oil under a landslide of
    dried chilies and Sichuan peppercorns, with a ladle already in it. It is not, perhaps, the lunch a careful person
    eats an hour before giving a talk.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'sichuan-fish.webp'} alt="A large white bowl of Sichuan fish in chili oil, covered in dried red chilies and spring onions, with a ladle." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lunch, mostly chilies.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The session was called <strong>Stable &amp; Accurate Elasticity</strong>: Tuesday, August 12th, 2 to 3:30 pm, West
    Building, Rooms 220–222. The opening slide listed six papers, and ours was number one, which meant there was no
    time to settle in by watching anyone else go first. Paper number four on that list was another one from our lab.
    A SIGGRAPH technical-papers room is a ballroom carved into smaller pieces, with a low stage, a podium with the
    conference logo, a projector screen in a black frame, and rows of chairs facing it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'session-slide.webp'} alt="A projected session slide titled Stable and Accurate Elasticity, Tuesday August 12, 2 to 3:30 pm, listing ANIME-Rod first." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The session's opening slide. Paper number one.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The paper was <strong>ANIME-Rod: Adjustable Nonlinear Isotropic Materials for Elastic Rods</strong>, by Jiahao Wen,
    Jernej Barbič and me, from the University of Southern California. The title slide put the name over a pair of
    braided, rainbow-colored rods twisting around each other, and the photos from the audience start there.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'talk-title.webp'} alt="A projected title slide reading ANIME-Rod: Adjustable Nonlinear Isotropic Materials for Elastic Rods, Huanyu Chen, Jiahao Wen, Jernej Barbič, University of Southern California, over braided colorful rods." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The title slide, Rooms 220–222.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    I won't try to give the talk again here, but the slides that were photographed tell its outline well enough. It
    opens with a volumetric simulation: a coiled, rainbow-shaded rod meshed in 3D, under the chain "3D volumetric mesh,
    deformation gradient, energy of the deformation gradient". That is the expensive, accurate way to simulate
    something long and thin. The talk then asks why it is hard for a rod model to match it, and works towards an answer
    on a slide titled "From 8D to 5D (Static Condensation)", where three of the eight variables, the ones
    describing how the cross-section deforms, are solved for and folded away, leaving a rod energy in five: stretch,
    twist, two curvatures and a cross-section scaling.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'talk-helix.webp'} alt="A slide with a colorful coiled rod and a table reading Volumetric Simulation, 3D Volumetric Mesh, Deformation Gradient." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Where it starts: a volumetric simulation.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'talk-condensation.webp'} alt="A slide titled From 8D to 5D (Static Condensation), with equations for stretch, bend and twist energies and a table of symbols." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"From 8D to 5D": static condensation.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two of the later slides are the fun ones. One compares two material models on a twisted rod curling into the kind
    of supercoil that is common in DNA molecules: under "Stable Neo-Hookean" and "Symmetric Dirichlet" the same rod ends
    up in visibly different shapes. The other is labeled "Application: Artistic Design", and shows what adjustable
    nonlinearity is for: a spinning ball of spiky magenta strands whose stretch response can be dialed up and down, and
    a row of little Y-shaped rods that bend and twist differently as their parameters change. Then the session chair
    stepped up beside the podium with a sheet of paper, and it was time for questions.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'talk-dna.webp'} alt="A slide comparing two twisted, supercoiled rods labeled Stable Neo-Hookean and Symmetric Dirichlet, under a heading mentioning DNA molecules." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Supercoiling, "common in DNA molecules".</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'talk-artistic.webp'} alt="A slide titled Application: Artistic Design, showing spiky magenta strand balls and a row of Y-shaped rods, labeled Nonlinear Stretchability and Nonlinear Bendability/Twistability." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"Application: Artistic Design".</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    There is a particular feeling to walking off that stage. For months a paper is a PDF, a set of reviews, a video and a
    deadline. For a few minutes in a carpeted room in Vancouver it becomes a thing you say out loud to people who
    came to hear it, and then it is done, and the next speaker is plugging in a laptop. The camera roll goes quiet for
    the rest of the afternoon, which seems about right.
  </p>

  <h2>Days 5–6: The rest of the week</h2>
  <p>
    Wednesday began at 9:13 in the morning at the top of a staircase in the West Building's atrium, under the wooden
    ceiling slats, looking down at a giant globe of the Earth hanging in the middle of the glass hall. Clouds and
    continents were printed on it at a scale that made it look like a planet seen from orbit, suspended above the
    escalators and the crowds of badge-wearers coming in for the day. Through the glass behind it, the towers of the
    financial district rose straight up from the street.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0814.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0814.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Days 5–6: Canada Place, the convention centre, West Hastings Street, the exhibition hall, and Yaletown." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 5–6: the last two days of the conference, and a move to Yaletown.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'atrium-globe.webp'} alt="A huge globe of the Earth suspended in the glass atrium of the convention centre, seen from the top of a staircase under a slatted wooden ceiling." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The globe in the West Building's atrium.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Two minutes later I was outside on the forecourt of <strong>Canada Place</strong>. From the land side it looks
    nothing like the postcard: instead of sails, a white wall of terminal and offices, a glass tower above it, a big
    maple leaf painted on a glass drum, and the red banners of the hop-on, hop-off buses. The sky had turned gray
    overnight, the first sign of the weather change that would see out the week. That afternoon, from across the
    street, I took the obligatory exterior of the <strong>Convention Centre West</strong> with the SIGGRAPH 2025
    banner over the entrance: a long, low glass building under a deep overhanging roof, its grass top just visible at the
    edge, with a steady stream of people crossing the plaza in front.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canada-place.webp'} alt="The land side of Canada Place: a white terminal building with a Canada Place sign, a glass tower, and a glass drum painted with a maple leaf." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Canada Place from the land side.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'vcc-west.webp'} alt="The Vancouver Convention Centre West building with a SIGGRAPH 2025 banner over the entrance, under a gray sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Vancouver Convention Centre West, with the SIGGRAPH banner.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The evening's reception food was better than conference food has any right to be: a slab of prime rib, pink all
    the way through, with grilled asparagus laid across it like a fence and a couple of roasted potatoes, followed by
    fruit skewers and sesame balls. These events are really about the conversations, and the food is mostly something
    to hold, but this time the food was worth a photo.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'prime-rib.webp'} alt="A plate of prime rib with grilled asparagus and roasted potatoes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Reception plate, Wednesday evening.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Thursday: the last day</h3>
  <p>
    Thursday, August 14th, was the conference's last day and my moving day, from near the convention centre to
    Yaletown for one more night downtown. The morning was gray and cool, and West Hastings Street had the look Vancouver
    is famous for under cloud: green-glass towers, dense and glossy, rising behind trees that are still lush in
    August. A bronze bird with outstretched wings stood on a plinth at
    the corner of Thurlow Street. A red double-decker sightseeing bus came by with "Vancouver" written across its side,
    in case anybody had forgotten.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hastings-towers.webp'} alt="Green glass office towers on West Hastings Street under a gray sky, with trees and a bronze bird sculpture on a plinth." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>West Hastings Street, Thursday morning.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Around noon I made a last pass through the <strong>exhibition hall</strong>, down in the lower level of the West
    Building. It is a dark, enormous room lit by rows of downlights, with company booths under hanging banners and a
    crowd that never thins. Sony and Dell banners hung over two of the biggest stands, and lines of people snaked
    across the floor in front of them. Near the entrance, the giant illuminated <strong>SIGGRAPH</strong> letters stood on a floor washed
    with blue and teal light, the most photographed object in the building. In Denver, a blue bear peered through the
    window behind them; in Vancouver, it was the letters alone.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'exhibit-hall.webp'} alt="A busy exhibition hall with crowds in front of Sony and Dell Technologies booths under rows of ceiling lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The exhibition hall at noon.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'siggraph-letters.webp'} alt="Large illuminated SIGGRAPH letters on a floor lit in blue and teal, with the exhibition behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The letters.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Lunch, eaten near Jack Poole Plaza according to the GPS, was a paper bowl of rice with
    fried chicken, kimchi, a boiled egg, spring onions, black and white sesame and a zigzag of chili sauce, and a cup of
    berries and melon. It is the kind of lunch you eat in fifteen minutes between two things you don't want to miss.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'lunch-bowl.webp'} alt="A paper bowl of rice with fried chicken, kimchi, egg, sesame and chili sauce, next to a cup of berries, on a gray wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Thursday lunch.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By six I was in <strong>Yaletown</strong>, high above the south end of downtown. Yaletown was a district of
    railway yards and brick warehouses a century ago, named for the railway workers who moved here in the 1880s from
    the town of Yale, up the Fraser Canyon; today it is a forest of glass
    and concrete residential towers, built mostly since the city hosted Expo 86 on the old rail lands along False Creek.
    From up there the view takes in a whole history of the city at once: the white, tent-like top of
    <strong>BC Place</strong>, the stadium whose cable-supported retractable roof replaced an inflated dome in 2011,
    and, in the corner, the curved stone wall of the Vancouver Public Library's Central Branch. Moshe Safdie's 1995
    library is famous for looking like the Colosseum, and from above you can see why: a great oval arcade wrapped
    around a rectangular block of books.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'yaletown-view.webp'} alt="A high view over Yaletown's residential towers under a gray sky, with the white roof of BC Place on the left and the curved wall of the Central Library in the lower corner." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Yaletown from above: BC Place on the left, the library's curved wall below.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner was Chinese takeout, spread out across a table: a coiled, flaky layered flatbread, a bowl of
    golden millet congee dotted with goji berries, a stir-fry of garlic scapes with wood ear mushrooms, and a dark,
    spicy braise. That night it rained, and the last photo of the day is of city lights blurred by water running down
    the glass. Vancouver had finally produced the weather it is known for, one day before I left downtown.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'takeout.webp'} alt="Takeout containers of a coiled flatbread, millet congee with goji berries, garlic scapes with wood ear mushrooms, and a spicy braise." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Takeout in Yaletown.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Days 7–8: A strike, and an extra day in Richmond</h2>
  <p>
    Friday, August 15th, was supposed to be the flight home. It wasn't. Air Canada's flight attendants, around ten
    thousand of them, were heading for their first strike in decades, and the airline had already begun winding
    down its schedule ahead of the deadline. My flight was one of the ones cancelled. The strike itself began in the
    early hours of the next morning, and for a few days the country's largest airline was almost entirely on the
    ground.
  </p>
  <p>
    So instead of an airport, Friday became a scramble. I needed a bed for the night at short notice, and found one in
    an Airbnb back in <strong>Richmond</strong>. A flight was harder. At first there was nothing to book at all, and
    once seats did appear they cost several times the original fare, because everyone else on every cancelled Air
    Canada flight was looking at the same screens. Eventually I got a seat on a Delta flight for Saturday: first class,
    since that was what was left, at a price I'd rather not repeat. Then, early on Saturday morning, a much cheaper
    flight turned up, still more than double what I'd originally paid, and I switched to it.
  </p>
  <p>
    In between, the camera roll records one errand, a new suitcase from an outdoor-gear store, and one dinner. It
    was Cantonese takeout this time: rice-noodle rolls, slippery and translucent with shrimp showing through, a tub of
    congee, and a box of deep-fried durian mochi balls. Bite into one and the crisp golden shell gives way to a chewy
    layer of glutinous rice and then a soft, pale-yellow center of durian, which is as divisive as fruit gets and
    which, in this form, even skeptics tend to forgive.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0816.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0816.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Days 7–8: from Richmond–Brighouse on the Canada Line to Waterfront and back to YVR, then takeoff over Richmond." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 7–8: an unplanned morning in Richmond, one last ride downtown on the Canada Line, then home.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'durian-mochi.webp'} alt="Golden fried durian mochi balls in a takeout box, one bitten open to show the yellow filling." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fried durian mochi, the unplanned last dinner.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The new flight left in the afternoon, which handed me something I hadn't planned on: a free morning in Richmond,
    a city I had mostly seen from the train and from takeout boxes. It started at the
    <strong>Richmond–Brighouse</strong> terminus of the Canada Line, on No. 3 Road. The sky had cleared into a mass of
    bright, piled-up clouds, and the guideway ran past on its concrete beam with a two-car train sitting at the
    station, white residential towers with blue and lime-green panels standing over it. Next to the station is
    Richmond Centre, the mall whose long glass barrel-vault roof turns the main concourse into something like a
    greenhouse. There was bubble tea, of course. It was Richmond.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'richmond-guideway.webp'} alt="An elevated Canada Line train at Richmond–Brighouse station under white residential towers and dramatic clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Richmond–Brighouse, the end of the line.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'richmond-mall.webp'} alt="Looking up at a long arched glass skylight roof in a shopping mall, with blue sky above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The glass roof at Richmond Centre.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then came the best seat in Vancouver's transit system. The <strong>Canada Line</strong> opened in 2009, just ahead
    of the Winter Olympics, and like the rest of the SkyTrain network it is fully automated, with no driver's cab. That
    means whoever boards first can stand at the very front and look straight down the track through a big window. The
    view from there is a roller coaster in slow motion. The guideway swings out ahead in a long curve on its concrete
    beam, past Capstan station and over the roofs and parking lots of Richmond, toward the river and the city. I
    stood there and watched it come.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canada-line-front.webp'} alt="The view from the front window of a driverless Canada Line train, looking down a curving elevated track toward buildings and clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The front window of a driverless Canada Line train.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    I rode it all the way to the end at <strong>Waterfront</strong>, half an hour from Richmond, for one last look
    at downtown. Waterfront Station was built in 1914 by the Canadian Pacific Railway as the western end of the
    transcontinental line, and its columned front still faces Cordova Street. Today it is the hub where the SkyTrain
    lines, the SeaBus to North Vancouver and the commuter trains all meet. Standing between its columns you see the
    city's two eras side by side: a stone office block from the railway age across the street, a curved glass tower
    beside it, and a tour coach at the curb in front. A few steps along West Cordova, the brick of Gastown
    begins.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waterfront-station.webp'} alt="The columns of Waterfront Station framing a street with a stone office building, glass towers and a tour coach." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From between the columns of Waterfront Station.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'west-cordova.webp'} alt="West Cordova Street with heritage stone and brick buildings, a glass tower, and a pedestrian overpass under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>West Cordova Street, at the edge of Gastown.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    It was a short visit. Three minutes on the street, by the timestamps, and then back underground and south again,
    a farewell lap rather than an outing. Half an hour later the train was crossing the Fraser's North Arm at
    Bridgeport. The <strong>North Arm Bridge</strong>, built for the Canada Line, carries the trains on a single
    concrete deck hung from short towers, with a walkway for bikes and pedestrians alongside. From the train, under a
    heavy sky, the new towers of Marpole lined up on the far bank.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'north-arm-bridge.webp'} alt="The North Arm of the Fraser River under heavy clouds, with a cable-supported bridge on the right and new towers on the far bank." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The North Arm Bridge and Marpole across the Fraser.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The airport did its usual job of making the city into merchandise. The shops in the departures area were stocked
    with cream "Vancouver" sweatshirts and an "Explore Canada" display of travel mugs, water bottles and bags printed
    with rows of little bears and maple leaves.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'yvr-souvenirs.webp'} alt="An Explore Canada souvenir display at the airport with patterned bags, travel mugs and water bottles." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Last chance for souvenirs, YVR.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Takeoff was a little after three. Taxiing out, the window showed the strike in a single frame: a long line of Air
    Canada jets parked nose to tail across a field of tall golden grass, going nowhere, with the mountains behind them
    under the clouds. Then the plane lifted off
    eastward over the delta, and below, the Middle Arm of the Fraser curled past new apartment blocks, a bridge and a
    stretch of empty fields.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'yvr-airliners.webp'} alt="A row of Air Canada jets parked across a field of golden grass, with clouds and mountains behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Air Canada's grounded fleet, on the first day of the strike.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'takeoff-middle-arm.webp'} alt="Aerial view after takeoff over the Middle Arm of the Fraser River, with a bridge, roads and apartment blocks." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Middle Arm, just after takeoff.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A moment later the whole of central Richmond was laid out like a circuit board, its long straight roads running
    out toward the water and the islands. Somewhere down there was the station where the morning had started.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'takeoff-richmond.webp'} alt="Aerial view over the grid of central Richmond, with the sea and islands on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Richmond from the air.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    That is where the record ends: an engine cowling, the delta, and the sea shining in the distance, a day later
    than planned.
  </p>

  <h2>A sequel</h2>
  <p>
    Put next to last year's trip, this one hardly counts as travel. In 2024, SIGGRAPH was the prologue, and the real
    story was two weeks of a different city almost every night. In 2025 there was no rental car and no road: one
    metro area, eight days, one train line, and a conference in the middle.
  </p>
  <p>
    But the camera roll makes a case for it anyway. A week in one place is a different kind of acquaintance from a city
    a day. You get to know the view from one set of windows well enough to notice that the ship outside is a different
    one every afternoon. You see the same mountains under a clear sky, on fire at sunset, and gone behind cloud. You
    stop checking which train goes where. And you find out that Richmond, which most visitors only see from the
    airport, is where a lot of the best food is.
  </p>
  <p>
    The strike didn't feel like a gift at the time. A cancelled flight, a night found at a few hours' notice and a
    morning spent refreshing fares are nobody's idea of a holiday. But it bought me a morning in Richmond that I would
    never have had otherwise, one more ride at the front of the train, and a lesson in how quickly a trip home can stop
    being something you can take for granted. Looking back, I'm glad of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    And then there is the reason for going. A year earlier I had stood on a show floor in Denver and photographed an
    ad for this conference. This time the photos that matter most aren't of mountains or ships at all. They are of a
    projector screen in Rooms 220–222, with a rod twisting on it and a title above our names. Somewhere on a show
    floor there will be a booth advertising the next one. I'll photograph that too.
  </p>
